        "original_id": "4f998e6a-5bf1-5794-a66c-bad306ef00df",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Johnny Majors, Paul Hornung, Mark Ingram Jr.\n- Organizations: Green Bay Packers, University of Alabama, Tennessee Volunteers, Southeastern Conference, Pro Football Hall of Fame\n- Places: Knoxville, Tennessee, Neyland Stadium\n- Awards: Heisman Trophy, Super Bowl, NFL titles, national championship\n- Roles: head coach, running back, professional American football player\n\nFacts:\n- In 1956, Johnny Majors finished second to Paul Hornung in Heisman Trophy voting.\n- Paul Hornung was born on December 23, 1935.\n- From 1957 to 1966, Paul Hornung played professional American football as a running back for the Green Bay Packers in the NFL.\n- Paul Hornung won four NFL titles and the first Super Bowl with the Green Bay Packers, and he is in the Pro Football Hall of Fame.\n- In 1990, Johnny Majors was the Tennessee Volunteers football team head coach in his 14th year as the team competed in the Southeastern Conference.\n- In 1990, the Tennessee Volunteers football team played its home games at Neyland Stadium in Knoxville, Tennessee.\n- Mark Ingram Jr. played college football for the University of Alabama, won the Heisman Trophy, and was a member of a national championship team.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "d75b8b78-c11b-5bdd-b555-859fdcff8586",
        "text": "Page: Johnny Majors. Sentence: He finished second to Paul Hornung in voting for the Heisman Trophy in 1956.\nPage: Paul Hornung. Sentence: Paul Vernon Hornung (born December 23, 1935), nicknamed \"The Golden Boy\", is a former professional American football player, a Hall of Fame running back for the Green Bay Packers of the National Football League (NFL) from 1957 to 1966, winning four NFL titles and the first Super Bowl.\nPage: 1990 Tennessee Volunteers football team. Sentence: Playing as a member of the Southeastern Conference (SEC), the team was led by head coach Johnny Majors, in his 14th year, and played their home games at Neyland Stadium in Knoxville, Tennessee.\nPage: Mark Ingram Jr.. Sentence: He played college football for the University of Alabama, won the Heisman Trophy, and was a member of a national championship team.",
